<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gonur Depe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2400–1600 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/25th-century-bce">25th century BCE</a> · Also known as Goňur depe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gonur Depe is the site of a large early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> settlement about 60 km north of Mary, ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a>, dated from 2400 to 1600 BCE and regarded as the major settlement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margiana">Margiana</a> Archaeological Complex. Viktor Sarianidi, who discovered it, excavated it from 1974 to 2013 and uncovered a palace, a fortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> enclosure and temples with fire altars, which he associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>. Its Royal Necropolis held gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and bronze goods and a foal buried on a four-wheeled wagon, radiocarbon-dated to 2250 BCE, and its Large Necropolis more than three thousand graves. The site was most likely abandoned after the Murghab River shifted to the west.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gonur-depe.jpg" alt="Gonur Depe" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Stanley · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gonur_ruins.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gonur Depe covers about 55 hectares in the delta of the Murghab River, where more than 150 ancient settlements have been found and where it is among the largest ruins. Archaeologists divide it into three sectors. Gonur North, an almost elliptical fortified area of about 330 by 460 metres, was occupied from about 2400 to 1900 BCE. At its centre stood the Monumental Palace, about 150 by 140 metres, inside a double wall, with two large courtyards, an axial corridor, storerooms and rooms that may have been a throne room and audience halls; water sources fed by ceramic pipes lay near its entrance.</p><p>About 200 metres west lies the Large Necropolis, some 10 hectares in extent. Only adults and young people over eight were buried there; children were buried near houses or in abandoned buildings. Gonur South, a smaller square complex of about 1.5 hectares, was lived in from about 1900 to 1600 BCE and was fortified by two rings of massive walls with round towers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Scholars think the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a> culture may have begun at sites such as Anau on the northern slopes of the Kopet-Dag, and that settlers moved from there to places like Gonur as the climate changed. Genetic study of people buried at Gonur finds that most of their ancestry resembles that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> farmers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian Plateau</a>; some outliers had South Asian-related ancestry, possibly through contact with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>. Ivory objects in the last phase of the Royal Necropolis are contemporary with late layers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altyndepe">Altyn Depe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohenjo-daro">Mohenjo-daro</a>. Camps of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronovo-culture">Andronovo culture</a> appeared at the site more often between 1800 and 1500 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Viktor Sarianidi first collected pottery sherds from the surface in 1972. He described a walled shrine in Gonur South, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temenos">Temenos</a>, as a precinct of the Soma cult and reported a vessel for preparing the drink with traces of cannabis, poppy and ephedra. Mosaics of the late third millennium BCE that decorated walls and wooden chests in the Necropolis have close parallels at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and in the Royal Cemetery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>. Archaeologist Julio Bendezu-Sarmiento holds that the foal and wagon burial predates similar Sintashta burials by about a century and a half. Stratigraphic studies from 2014 to 2018 by Robert M. Sataev, Nadezhda A. Dubova and others identified five building periods.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gonur Depe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Gonur Depe</b>, in the Murghab delta of southern Turkmenistan, was the largest settlement, about 55 hectares, and Sarianidi regarded it as the “capital” of Margiana.</blockquote><a href="/culture/oxus-civilisation">Oxus civilisation · Society</a></li></ul></section>
